<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdul Fattah Ismail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Abd al-Fattah Ismail</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdul Fattah Ismail was a Yemeni Marxist politician, the chief ideologue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>. Born in North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, he led the party's pro-Soviet wing, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a> in 1978 and headed the state from 1978 to 1980, when he was forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. He returned in 1985 and was killed in the fighting of January 1986 between his supporters and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-fattah-ismail.jpg" alt="Abdul Fattah Ismail" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdulfatah-1979-188.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdul Fattah Ismail was born on 28 July 1939 in the Al-Hujariah district of Taiz Governorate. His father was a faqih, an Islamic jurist, but the family was poor and rural. He followed his elder brother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, then a British protectorate, and studied at the Ahliah School in Tawahi. From 1956 to 1959 he was an apprentice at an oil refinery after training at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">British Petroleum</a> centre, and there he took up union organising. A co-founder of the Arab Nationalist Movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>, he was arrested by the British for inciting workers, and from 1961 he taught at Al-Haswah School in Aden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-emergency">Aden Emergency</a> led its underground fedayeen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. After independence in 1967 he became Minister of Culture and Yemeni Unity. Arrested by the party's right wing in March 1968, he went into exile and drafted a leftist manifesto, the program for Accomplishing National Democratic Liberation. The left wing regained power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrective-move">Corrective Move</a> of 22 June 1969, after which he became Secretary General of the NLF Central Committee and the country's de facto leader.</p><p>He led the talks with other left parties that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a> and was elected its Secretary General at the first congress in October 1978. Favouring the Soviet model of socialist development, he secured the Treaty of Friendship and Cooperation with the USSR in October 1979. In 1980 he resigned all his posts, officially for health reasons, was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a> and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> for medical treatment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned in 1985 as a crisis grew between Ali Nasir and his opponents, and in October he was elected to the party's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. On 13 January 1986 the crisis broke into fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> that lasted more than a month, caused thousands of casualties and ended with Ali Nasir's ouster. Abdul Fattah was last seen being evacuated from the party office in an armoured vehicle with his deputy Ali Salem al-Beidh. Reports, never substantiated, say naval forces loyal to Ali Nasir shelled his home, and his body was never found.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdul Fattah Ismail” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Qahtan al-Shaabi, was removed in June 1969 in what the NLF’s left wing called the Corrective Move, and power passed to a collective leadership including Salim Rubaya Ali as head of the Presidential Council, the party ideologue <b>Abdul Fattah Ismail</b> and later Ali Nasir Muhammad as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Al Aghābirah, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.31,44.29&amp;year=1969" aria-label="Al Aghābirah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5764 1668 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abdul Fattah Ismail"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6214 1938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6214 1938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.31,44.29&amp;year=1969">What was here in 1969? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
